name 'pl-aif1-js-variables-operations'
title 'title'
description 'description here'

markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h1>Creating and Updating Variables in JavaScript</h1>

<p>Here's a variable being created, then updated by an operation, then displayed on the page:</p>

<div style="background-color: #2B2745; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 22px; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 15px; color: #E4E2F8; line-height: 1.8; margin: 20px 0;">
let score = 0;<br>
score = score + 1;<br>
scoreDisplay.textContent = score;
</div>

<p>Line one creates the variable and gives it a starting value. Line two is the operation, it changes that value. Line three updates the DOM so the new value actually shows up on the page, the same DOM update process from the last lesson.</p>

<h2>Two Ways to Create Variables</h2>

<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
  <div>
    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 8px;">
      <span style="width: 12px; height: 12px; border-radius: 3px; background-color: #4C42CF; display: inline-block;"></span>
      <span style="font-weight: 700; color: #1F1976; font-size: 15px;">let — values that change</span>
    </div>
    <div style="background-color: #2B2745; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 14px; color: #E4E2F8;">
      let score = 4;<br>let username = "GamerGirl";
    </div>
  </div>
  <div>
    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 8px;">
      <span style="width: 12px; height: 12px; border-radius: 3px; background-color: #ACA8EA; display: inline-block;"></span>
      <span style="font-weight: 700; color: #1F1976; font-size: 15px;">const — values that stay the same</span>
    </div>
    <div style="background-color: #2B2745; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 14px; color: #E4E2F8;">
      const maxLives = 3;<br>const gameTitle = "Space Quest";
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The keyword itself is a signal, not just the name. <code>score</code> uses <code>let</code> because it's expected to go up as the game is played. <code>gameTitle</code> uses <code>const</code> because it's set once and never needs to change while the page is open. This isn't just a naming convention: if code tries to reassign a value declared with <code>const</code>, JavaScript throws an error and stops. <code>const</code> isn't a suggestion, it's the language enforcing a promise that the value won't change.</p>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; color: #222222; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
<p><strong>💡 Teacher tip:</strong> a helpful test for students deciding between the two: if the value should ever update while the app runs, use let. If it should stay fixed for the life of the page, use const. When AI generates code, this choice is already made for them, but recognizing it helps them read and trust what AI produced.</p>
</div>

<p>Next, you'll practice reading AI-generated code the way a QA Engineer would: not just checking whether it runs, but whether it actually does what was asked.</p>
MARKDOWN
